  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   The central idea is to minimize the maximum overlap of a step function \\( h(x) \\) defined on [0,2] with itself at shifts, subject to \\(\\int_{0}^{2} h(x) dx = 1\\) and \\(h(x) \\in [0,1]\\). The challenge is to escape metastable states in complex optimization landscapes. This is achieved by leveraging:  \n   - **Parent construction transfer**: Starting optimization on coarser grids (n=47,94,188) adapted from the parent solution.  \n   - **Randomized iterative refinement**: Exploring configurational space via targeted perturbations and gradient projection using the F\u00f6rster potential derived from the overlap integral.  \n   - **Adaptive gradient climbing**: Rapidly climbing objectives in curvilinear coordinates using matrix-aware gradient descent.  \n   - **Constrained optimization**: Ensuring feasibility with projection-based updates diachronicpectral to phase transitions.  \n\n   Limitations include:  \n   - The approach is derivative-based (gradient descent) and thus local attractors, though fallbacks are prepared.  \n   - Dependence on convergence: High-quality initialization ensures rapid descent from solutions.  \n   - Computational complexity: \\(O(n^2)\\) operations per update, necessitating grid size ~94.  \n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   Representation:  \n   - \\( h \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n} \\), with \\(dx=2/n\\) and \\(\\sum h_i = n/2\\).  \n   - Feasibility: \\(0 \\le h_i \\le 1\\), enforced via projection.  \n\n   Objective:  \n   \\[\n   C_5 = \\max_k \\left( \\frac{2}{n} \\cdot \\left\\langle h \\circ (1 - \\sigma_{d,\\parallel}(h - k)) \\right\\rangle_{\\text{dot}} \\right)\n   \\]\n   Where \\(\\sigma_{d,\\parallel}\\) is a directional projection operator and \\(\\circ\\) denotes Hadamard product for grid-aligned operations. \n\n   Constraints:  \n   - Integral: \\(\\sum h_i = n/2\\)  \n   - Bounds: \\(0 \\le h_i \\le 1\\)  \n\n   Optimization variables: \\(h(x)\\) coefficients.  \n\n   Implementation uses matrix multiplication for gradient evaluation (F\u00f6rster potential first-order approximation) and projected gradient descent (method='SLSQP' in scipy) with the equality constraint handled numerically.  \n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   - **Initialization**: Start with parent \\(n=47\\), then double for higher grids.  \n   - **Design variables**: Continuous variables \\(h_i \\in [0,1]\\).  \n   - **Objective function**:  \n     \\[\n     C_5(h) = \\frac{2}{n} \\cdot \\max_k \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h_i \\cdot (1 - h_{i+k}) \\right)\n     \\]\n     computed via matrix correlation.  \n   - **Gradient approximation**:  \n     \\[\n     \\nabla C_5(h) \\approx dx \\cdot \\sum_{k \\in \\text{max}_k} \\left[ (1 - h_{i+k}) - h_{i-k} \\right]\n     \\]\n     for active lags \\(k\\).  \n   - **Core loop**:  \n     - Begin at initial guess.  \n     - Compute forager potential (objective) and gradient.  \n     - Update:  \n       \\[\n       h_{\\text{new}} = \\Pi_{h} \\left( h - \\alpha \\cdot \\nabla C_5(h) \\right)\n       \\]\n       where \\(\\Pi\\) ensures bounds and integral.  \n   - **Termination**: Stop if time exceeds budget or stable plateau.  \n\n4. Search choices and variations  \n   - **Grid doubling**: n *= 2 as fallback.  \n   - **Random skittering**: Add random state noise to escape saddle basins.  \n   - **Gradient frequency**: Alternate gradient evaluations (with random masks).  \n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   Time per grid:  \n   - \u2019n_val=47': ~10s, \u2019n_val=94': ~200s,  careers n_val=188': >800s.  \n   Use time monitoring: total optimization time under budget.  \n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   - **Feasibility Check**: Verify \\(\\sum h_i\\) within 0.5 of \\(n/2\\).  \n   - **Objective recompute**: Use only the low-level matrix operations.  \n   - **Fallback**: Return parent solution if local maxima.",
